WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:04.810
TIM SWAST: Hello.

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:06.430
I'm Tim Swast.

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:10.900
I'm an engineer on the Google
Developer Relation team.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.360
I write code samples
and tutorials, mostly

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.490
focusing on Google's
big data products.

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:20.350
So speaking of data,
let's take a look at some.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:22.570
It's a bunch of numbers.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:24.820
Can you see any patterns here?

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.640
Probably not, because this
is only 10% of the data.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.600
So let me bring it
all on one slide.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.530
Now it should be really
clear what's going on.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:35.360
Well, it's not clear to me.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:36.640
That's because I'm a human.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.650
I can't ingest raw numbers.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:40.900
I need a human-friendly
visualization

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.720
to figure out what's going
on and make inferences

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:44.500
about my data.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:50.950
The data that we're
going to see in this demo

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:54.130
is mostly going to
be from BigQuery.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:59.560
BigQuery is a petabyte-scale,
server-less data warehouse.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.380
It gives you
standard equal access

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:05.570
to very large scale data.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:10.480
Now, if you store your data
in something like Cloud SQL,

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:15.550
you can still get the same
power of the BigQuery SQL engine

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:19.240
using BigQuery's
federated query feature.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.950
And now, we've also launched
the Bigtable federated query

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:27.100
feature, so if you've got a
bunch of streaming IoT data

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:30.040
into Cloud Bigtable,
you can access that

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:34.000
through the same SQL
interface from BigQuery.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.240
And we're also going
to see a little bit

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.840
of connecting visualization
tools to Cloud SQL as well.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:46.190
I figure you don't want to
just look at random data,

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:49.780
so let's look at some
real world stuff today.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:53.770
We're going to use Google's
public dataset program and just

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:56.110
a handful of data
sets from that--

00:01:56.110 --> 00:02:00.730
some weather data, some
data from various US cities,

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:02.770
and the USA names database.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:05.480
But there are many more
datasets available,

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:07.800
and Google's adding
more all the time.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:12.450
And for the tools that
we're going to use,

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:14.990
we're going to see a bit
of Cloud Datalab, which

00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:19.280
is a Python environment for
building visualizations,

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.750
and doing some machine
learning tasks.

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:26.050
We're going to build some
dashboards with Data Studio.

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:29.734
And no visualization talk
is complete without talking

00:02:29.734 --> 00:02:31.150
about spreadsheets,
so we're going

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:33.100
to use Google Sheets
a little bit today.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:34.900
And then Tableau is
going to come on stage

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:39.190
and demonstrate their
powerful visualization tool.

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:42.880
Google Cloud connects to many
other visualization tools

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.400
via our partner companies.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.210
A few of them are listed here.

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:50.080
And many open source tools
let you connect easily

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:50.840
to Google Cloud.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.580
You're going to see
a lot of code today.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.580
I don't want you to worry too
much about understanding it,

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.860
because I'm going to zip
through it pretty fast.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.450
I made a blog post here.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:07.470
You can go to that link,
bit.ly/visualize-with-gcp,

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:11.020
and I've got links to all the
demos I've done with source

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:11.520
code.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.690
So why are we
visualizing our data?

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.270
The big reason to
visualize your data

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:22.870
is you've got a problem
you want to solve.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:27.050
So one problem that
a lot of people have

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:28.790
is how to name a child.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:31.940
Now, that's a very
personal decision,

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:34.310
but I'm a geek so I
want to try to use

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:36.050
big data to help solve this.

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:43.090
The first thing to do when
you're building a visualization

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:45.340
is to build a
summary of the data.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:48.700
It doesn't make sense to try
to see gigabytes and gigabytes

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.590
of data all in one plot.

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:54.170
We want to build a nice
summary and visualize that.

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:56.950
So the way we're going to
do that is with BigQuery.

00:03:56.950 --> 00:04:01.600
So for our name
assignment task, we

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.110
might want to try to get a feel
for what names are out there

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:07.060
and how are they distributed.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:10.030
So this query selects
the first letter

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:13.090
of all the names in
the USA names database

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:17.260
and counts how many names
start with that letter.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:19.300
Now let's cut to the demo.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:23.390
This is that same query,
but in the BigQuery UI.

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:26.790
I'm going to click this red
button here to run the query,

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:29.650
and that's going to
take a few seconds.

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:33.930
And once that
completes, we're going

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:35.730
to click this Save
to Google Sheets

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:36.920
button in the right corner.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:42.600
And then we'll
click to view that.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:47.160
It's opened a new tab, and
then once that loads up we're

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:51.320
going to see the results of
our query in a Google Sheet.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:55.160
Now, I don't care too much
about the exact numbers.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.020
What I really want is to get
a feel for the distribution,

00:04:58.020 --> 00:04:59.540
so I'm going to
insert a new chart.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:07.090
Google Sheets recommends, based
on the types of data it sees,

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:08.890
different visualizations.

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:10.720
In this case it's
suggesting a bar chart,

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:13.750
which is exactly what we want.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:16.630
Let's insert that.

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:21.010
Now we can clearly see that
the letter J on the left

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:25.450
is by far the most popular
starting letter for names.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:28.570
And then it trails
off slowly, eventually

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.900
getting down to the letter
U, which is so infrequently

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:35.680
used it doesn't even show up
as a bar on this bar chart.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.220
So maybe if you're
going for a unique name,

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:39.720
pick one that starts
with the letter

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:43.060
U. Ursula or Ulysses might
be a good option there.

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:51.036
We have explored our data
by building a visualization.

00:05:51.036 --> 00:05:52.660
And the great thing
about Google Sheets

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:55.000
is it's easy to share
it with your coworkers,

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.400
so they can see both
the results of the query

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:02.430
and the visualizations
you've built.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:06.030
Now, there's a potential
problem with picking

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:09.690
a name that starts with the
letter U. I know I experienced

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:12.270
this in elementary school.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:15.510
So you're back in
elementary school,

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:18.570
and you're lining up to
go outside for recess.

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:21.600
The teacher's going to
line you up alphabetically.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.350
And it's really uncomfortable
being at the end of the line,

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:27.540
and it's also
equally uncomfortable

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:30.660
being at the
beginning of the line.

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:34.830
So I know growing up, I wanted
to be in that nice, comfortable

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:36.090
center spot.

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:40.260
So let's see if we can build a
visualization to help optimize

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:43.260
for that in our name.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:45.680
So to summarize the
data, we're going

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:49.240
to run a different SQL query.

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:52.950
In this case, we're looking at
all the names in the dataset

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:56.680
and counting how many people
total have had that name.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:59.760
And that might seem like a
lot of data-- and it is--

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:02.700
but it's a small enough set
that our visualization tools can

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:06.450
handle it and it lets us
filter it in different ways

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:08.280
after we've run
our initial query.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:14.790
This is Cloud Datalab.

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:18.640
It's a Python/Jupiter
notebook environment,

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:21.240
and what that means
is it's built up

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:25.210
of these gray boxes
which are called cells.

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:28.170
So these cells have
Python code in them,

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:30.720
and you see they're
highlighted in gray right now.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:32.850
That means I haven't
run them yet.

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:34.470
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:36.360
to click through
and hit the Shift

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:38.370
and Enter key on my
keyboard, and that's

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:40.062
going to run the code live.

00:07:40.062 --> 00:07:41.520
So the first thing
that we're doing

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:43.920
is importing some libraries.

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:47.640
Those libraries might be
familiar to many Python

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:48.660
programmers.

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:50.820
We're just using
matplotlib, which

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:54.060
is very common for building
visualizations in Python,

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:56.400
and pandas for
doing our analysis

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:00.580
and also doing our
BigQuery query.

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:02.840
So our query completed.

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:05.060
And you see there's
about 30,000 rows here,

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:07.980
so let's build a chart of that.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:10.170
This is called a Pareto chart.

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:12.960
It's basically a bar
chart, because you

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:16.980
can see the bars here very much
like the previous plot we saw.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:20.250
But then there's this
extra line graph here,

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:21.990
and what that line
graph represents

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:24.150
is the cumulative total.

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:26.360
So, for example,
at the letter I,

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:28.950
you see it crosses the 40% mark.

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:33.146
That means 40% of names begin
with the letters A through I--

00:08:33.146 --> 00:08:37.650
so anything at I or
appearing before it.

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:42.030
Now, what we're looking for is
that nice center spot, so we're

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:44.790
looking for where it
crosses the 50% mark, which

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:51.710
seems to be at about the letter
J. So let's zoom in on that.

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:54.440
So here's another Pareto plot,
this time with the first two

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:57.890
letters of the different
names, this time starting

00:08:57.890 --> 00:08:59.630
with the letter J.

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:01.370
And we're looking
for that 50% mark

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:05.990
again, so we've got the
letters J, O. So maybe start

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:07.490
a name that begins with Jo.

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:13.180
So zoom in again, and, again,
look for that 50% mark line.

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:15.880
And we see it crosses at jor.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:17.980
Now, for our final
visualization,

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:19.670
we're actually
going to do a table.

00:09:19.670 --> 00:09:23.680
And the table is technically a
visualization, but in this case

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:25.060
it is the most appropriate one.

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:28.000
Because we're looking
for a specific name,

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:32.230
so we're looking for
that exact 50% mark,

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:33.610
right where it crosses.

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:35.950
And you see there's
some pretty close ties,

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:40.990
but Jordan is our
perfectly unbiased name.

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:43.150
If you pick the
name Jordan, you're

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:44.860
going to have the
best chance of being

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:48.080
at the center of those
lines in elementary school.

00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:54.820
So that was a bit
silly, so let's get back

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:58.390
to some serious data.

00:09:58.390 --> 00:10:00.550
This actually does
mean something.

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:04.030
This represents the last
10 years of weather data

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:06.360
from Austin, Texas.

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:09.670
So Austin's famous for many
things and many festivals,

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:14.650
but one of those is the Austin
City Limits Music Festival.

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:18.880
So this festival has been
going on for over 10 years,

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:22.750
and a few years ago
back in 2009 they

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:25.180
switched from being on
a weekend, primarily

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:28.960
in September, to a
weekend in October.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:32.570
Now, my Austin friends have
many theories about this,

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:35.230
but the most common
one seems to be

00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:38.990
it's really hot in
Austin in September,

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:42.220
especially for an outdoor
music festival where you're out

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:44.020
in the sun all day.

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:50.050
So my friends' hypothesis is
that the organizers switch

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:52.800
to October to beat the heat.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:54.810
Now, that's a
great theory and it

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:56.610
seems to make some
intuitive sense,

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:59.070
but let's use some
big data and see

00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:02.280
if this hypothesis is correct.

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:04.500
The first thing we're going
to do is build a summary,

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:07.780
so we're going to run
another BigQuery query.

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:13.840
This query selects data from
the NOAA weather public dataset.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:17.470
So that data set is over 100
years of temperature data

00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:19.790
from weather stations
across the world.

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:21.550
So the way we're going
to filter it down

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:24.730
is just select the Austin,
Texas, weather station

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:27.220
and limit to just the past
10 years of weather data.

00:11:30.830 --> 00:11:34.250
Again, we're going to
use Datalab Notebook

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:38.177
and the same libraries,
pandas matplotlib.

00:11:38.177 --> 00:11:39.760
And this query might
take a little bit

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:42.580
longer because it's a
slightly larger dataset,

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:45.850
but it's still pretty fast.

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:49.310
Now let's plot this data out.

00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:51.830
Now, this is the
exact same set of data

00:11:51.830 --> 00:11:56.620
that you saw in that slide,
but it should be a lot clearer,

00:11:56.620 --> 00:11:58.360
the patterns here.

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:01.510
So I've taken 10 years
of data, and then I've

00:12:01.510 --> 00:12:03.460
overlaid them on
top of each other

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:05.830
so that you can see
the seasonal changes.

00:12:05.830 --> 00:12:08.680
So the x-axis here is
the day of the year,

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:11.614
and the y-axis is the
temperature in Fahrenheit.

00:12:11.614 --> 00:12:13.030
And red means the
high temperature

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:15.340
for the day, blues
the low temperature,

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:18.320
and gray is the average
temperature for the day.

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:20.470
So one thing you can
definitely see here

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:23.890
is it's a lot hotter in
summer, as we'd expect,

00:12:23.890 --> 00:12:27.460
and actually the variance
between temperatures

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:30.310
is a lot lower in the summer
months versus the winter

00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:31.220
months.

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:36.010
They really change throughout
the days in the winter.

00:12:36.010 --> 00:12:38.500
But this graph doesn't
answer our question.

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:41.680
What we want to know is
where music festivals

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:44.360
lie on this line.

00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:45.740
Let's see that.

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:47.510
I've zoomed in a little bit.

00:12:47.510 --> 00:12:50.900
On the left is one
of the music festival

00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:54.200
weekends that happened in
September, and on the right

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:56.660
the blue line is one
of the music festivals

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:58.460
that happened in October.

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:00.710
So we can definitely see
a downward trend here.

00:13:00.710 --> 00:13:05.030
There's about five degrees
difference between October

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:07.820
and September and
the high temperatures

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:11.240
and low temperatures,
but five degrees

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:13.710
is not a big difference.

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:16.880
So maybe that's not
the primary reason

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:22.520
the organizers picked
October as the month.

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:26.300
One thing about choosing
October is the first year

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:31.110
that the Austin City Limits
Music Festival was in October,

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:33.500
there were torrential
downpours all weekend.

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:37.490
So this outdoor music festival
was a really muddy mess,

00:13:37.490 --> 00:13:40.610
and they had spread
composts out on the lawn

00:13:40.610 --> 00:13:43.520
before and so it
smelled really bad, too.

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:47.120
So maybe if you're
picking an ideal weekend

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:51.020
based on the weather for
your outdoor music festival,

00:13:51.020 --> 00:13:53.370
maybe pick it on more than
just the temperature data.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:01.590
So speaking of Austin, I used to
live there for about five years

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:02.940
or so.

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:05.640
And one of the problems
I encountered, and wish

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:08.460
I had a visualization
to help me solve,

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:10.920
was choosing where to live.

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:12.900
I'm a big public transit nerd.

00:14:12.900 --> 00:14:17.010
I like to take the bus and
the light rail whenever I can.

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:19.200
Even in Austin where
it's 100 degrees out

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:24.090
and I'm outside in the hot
sun, I'll still take the bus.

00:14:24.090 --> 00:14:27.930
So maybe my ideal spot would
be close to a reliable public

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:30.180
transit station.

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:32.100
The other thing about
living in Austin

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:33.810
is you learn to love the taco.

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:36.390
You end up eating tacos
every meal of the day,

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:38.606
but especially breakfast.

00:14:38.606 --> 00:14:41.440
So my ideal spot
would be somewhere

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:44.050
that's both close
to reliable transit

00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:48.000
but also close to some of
my favorite taco shops.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:53.100
So let's build a visualization
to help us solve this problem.

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:55.030
We're going to summarize
the data again,

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:57.490
again with BigQuery.

00:14:57.490 --> 00:15:00.100
This time we're going
to query a dataset

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:02.740
called cap metrics,
which was uploaded

00:15:02.740 --> 00:15:05.550
by a member of the Open Data
community, Sean Cascketta

00:15:05.550 --> 00:15:09.190
to a public BigQuery dataset.

00:15:09.190 --> 00:15:13.010
And this data includes
bus tracking data--

00:15:13.010 --> 00:15:17.260
so times and locations of all
the buses, as well as the bus

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:18.830
stops.

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:21.610
So what I'm selecting
here is whenever

00:15:21.610 --> 00:15:25.630
a bus is at a bus stop, I want
to know is that bus on schedule

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:29.830
or off schedule, because I'm
using that as my measure of how

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:31.240
reliable the buses are.

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:35.650
I'd rather the bus be on
schedule than off schedule.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:40.520
So back to the demo.

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:44.130
This is another notebook, and
this is more Python code here.

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:48.680
I'm using pandas and matplotlib,
but a few other libraries here

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:50.780
to work with geographic data.

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:54.380
And also, I uploaded
the query results

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:58.710
to a Cloud SQL
postgres instance.

00:15:58.710 --> 00:16:04.100
The reason I did that is because
the postgres instance supports

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:07.160
the PostGIS extension,
which allows me

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:10.730
to do GIS queries against it.

00:16:10.730 --> 00:16:14.330
In this case, I've got
my bus tracking data,

00:16:14.330 --> 00:16:18.590
and I also added a table
for my favorite taco shops.

00:16:18.590 --> 00:16:26.490
So I'm selecting the distance to
the closest taco shop via SQL.

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:29.080
So the query is finished.

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:31.920
Let's build a plot of this.

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:35.520
This is called a
Voronoi diagram.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:38.010
It gives you an idea of
where the bus stops are--

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:40.230
that's these blue dots here--

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:42.960
but also the area
served by that bus stop.

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:45.525
These blue dots are
surrounded each by a polygon.

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:52.140
Any point within that
area, the nearest bus stop

00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:55.760
is the blue circle
contained within it.

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:59.140
Now, I've plotted this
latitude-longitude data

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:03.040
on just a regular xy
plane, and that's not

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:04.250
quite the right thing to do.

00:17:04.250 --> 00:17:06.970
So let's plot it out
on a map instead.

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:11.136
So I'm going to convert this
Voronoi diagram into Go-JSON,

00:17:11.136 --> 00:17:12.760
and then I'm going
to plot it out using

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:15.069
a library called leaflet.

00:17:15.069 --> 00:17:21.550
So here's all our bus stop
areas plotted out on a map.

00:17:21.550 --> 00:17:22.960
So we can see the
different areas

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:28.640
served by the bus stops
overlaid on the map of Austin.

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:30.740
This doesn't quite
answer our question,

00:17:30.740 --> 00:17:35.540
so let me color it in
based on the reliability

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:38.060
of the buses in those stations.

00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:41.720
So now we can see the dark
purple areas are where

00:17:41.720 --> 00:17:45.170
the buses are not so reliable.

00:17:45.170 --> 00:17:49.400
They're off-schedule on
average by quite a bit,

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:52.370
and the lighter areas are
where the public transit is

00:17:52.370 --> 00:17:57.150
pretty reliable and they're
on schedule most of the time.

00:17:57.150 --> 00:17:57.890
So that's great.

00:17:57.890 --> 00:18:00.170
We could use this to
find an apartment,

00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:02.360
but it doesn't solve
my taco problem.

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:08.860
So let's combine the taco
table with this data set.

00:18:08.860 --> 00:18:12.120
So now I've overlaid
where all my favorite taco

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:14.280
shops are, and then
I've colored it

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:20.380
in not only by the public
transit reliability,

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:23.880
but also the proximity
to a taco shop.

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:28.140
So the light areas are the
areas that have reliable transit

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:31.620
and are close to a taco
shop, and the darker areas

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:38.620
are either not reliable or far
away from a taco shop or both.

00:18:38.620 --> 00:18:43.500
So panning around here, we
can see a few possible choices

00:18:43.500 --> 00:18:45.720
if we want to live
on the south side.

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:48.600
I happen to know this is
William Canyon Road down here,

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:51.270
and it's by Torchy's Tacos.

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:52.830
If we want to live
on the east side,

00:18:52.830 --> 00:18:54.720
there's not too many
choices, but we've

00:18:54.720 --> 00:19:00.150
got a little spot of white here
by the Plaza Saltillo light

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:02.820
rail station and El Chilito.

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:05.010
Or if we want to live
on the north side,

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:09.240
there's a nice spot up
here across Torchy's Tacos

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:12.180
on Burnet Road.

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:14.730
So we found the
perfect place to live.

00:19:14.730 --> 00:19:19.255
It's got reliable public transit
and proximity to delicious

00:19:19.255 --> 00:19:19.755
tacos.

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:27.000
So let's imagine for a minute
that you're a data scientist

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:29.680
and you're working for the
public transit company.

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:32.130
So you just saw my
talk and you want

00:19:32.130 --> 00:19:35.880
to cut down on the number
of those purple areas.

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:40.150
So it's your job to help keep
these buses running on time.

00:19:40.150 --> 00:19:42.810
So you're going to do the
same kinds of analysis

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:45.210
that we just saw,
but eventually you're

00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:49.140
going to want to share your
work with one of your coworkers.

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:51.660
So one way to do that
is to take a screenshot

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:54.030
of your visualization
and then send it off

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:56.960
in an email to your coworker.

00:19:56.960 --> 00:19:58.190
Now, that works fine.

00:19:58.190 --> 00:20:00.920
They're going to see the
exact same image you saw,

00:20:00.920 --> 00:20:02.600
but there's a problem with that.

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:04.160
Buses move around.

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:07.190
So by the time your coworker
gets to that email an hour

00:20:07.190 --> 00:20:10.790
later, the problem
will have evolved.

00:20:10.790 --> 00:20:14.000
So ideally we'd have
a live dashboard that

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:18.870
shows not the data an hour
ago, but the data as it

00:20:18.870 --> 00:20:21.600
is right now, and we're going
to use Data Studio to build

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:24.450
a dashboard like that.

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:27.840
Data Studio allows you
to connect to BigQuery,

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:33.270
as we've seen in the other
visualizations in this talk.

00:20:33.270 --> 00:20:36.960
But it also supports Google
Analytics and many other data

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:37.590
sources.

00:20:37.590 --> 00:20:40.600
In this case, we're going
to use a BigQuery query.

00:20:40.600 --> 00:20:44.340
So this query is very similar
to the previous one we saw.

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:47.250
I'm still looking at
how far off-schedule

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:49.290
the buses are on average,
but this time I'm

00:20:49.290 --> 00:20:52.480
splitting up by the day of the
week and the hour of the day

00:20:52.480 --> 00:20:56.760
because I want to see how the
problem changes over time.

00:20:56.760 --> 00:21:04.590
Cutting to the demo, I built
this Data Studio dashboard.

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:07.260
So I used Data Studio's
filtering mechanism

00:21:07.260 --> 00:21:10.290
to build seven
different graphs here,

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:12.900
each showing a different
day of the week.

00:21:12.900 --> 00:21:16.110
And then I've got a
line graph for each one.

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:18.420
The x-axis is the
time of the day,

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:21.750
and the y-axis is how far
off-schedule are the buses

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:24.090
on average for that hour.

00:21:24.090 --> 00:21:26.350
So you can see the buses
start off on time--

00:21:26.350 --> 00:21:28.620
which makes sense; they've
just started their route--

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:30.600
and then they get more
and more off-schedule

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:31.790
throughout the day.

00:21:31.790 --> 00:21:34.120
In the afternoon, they
catch up a little bit,

00:21:34.120 --> 00:21:37.450
but then rush hour hits and
they get way out of whack.

00:21:37.450 --> 00:21:39.600
So the great thing
about Data Studio

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:41.640
is this Share button up here.

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:42.660
You can click that.

00:21:42.660 --> 00:21:43.860
It gives you a link.

00:21:43.860 --> 00:21:46.710
You can send that
off to your coworker,

00:21:46.710 --> 00:21:48.570
and then when they
load this page up

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:50.340
it's going to rerun
that query and they'll

00:21:50.340 --> 00:21:52.500
see a life view of the data.

00:21:52.500 --> 00:21:55.260
And if you want their help
building this dashboard-- maybe

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:58.290
you want them to add some
new visualizations to it--

00:21:58.290 --> 00:22:00.000
you can give them
edit access, just

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:02.590
like you would a Google Doc.

00:22:02.590 --> 00:22:05.770
Now, I've only used line
graphs in this dashboard,

00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:10.870
but Data Studio also supports
bar charts, geographic maps,

00:22:10.870 --> 00:22:14.030
scatter charts, all the
visualizations we saw today

00:22:14.030 --> 00:22:14.530
and more.

00:22:19.330 --> 00:22:22.780
Data Studio's great for
this sharing use case.

00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:25.390
It's not the tool I'd
use for the analysis

00:22:25.390 --> 00:22:28.840
part, the exploration
phase, figuring out

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:31.180
what transformations and
I want to make to the data

00:22:31.180 --> 00:22:32.920
and how I want to visualize it.

00:22:32.920 --> 00:22:35.830
For that, I like to
use those notebooks.

00:22:35.830 --> 00:22:39.010
It's really handy to be
able to run some Python

00:22:39.010 --> 00:22:42.670
code to grab some data,
filter it in a bunch of ways,

00:22:42.670 --> 00:22:45.760
and try out different
visualizations.

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:49.240
So speaking of
code, again, here's

00:22:49.240 --> 00:22:51.010
the link to the
blog post in case

00:22:51.010 --> 00:22:53.890
you forgot it and you
want to look up the code I

00:22:53.890 --> 00:22:56.050
used to build these demos.

00:22:56.050 --> 00:22:59.630
But if you're not a coder,
there's another option,

00:22:59.630 --> 00:23:00.660
and that's Tableau.

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:03.220
Tableau is a powerful
visualization and analysis

00:23:03.220 --> 00:23:04.360
tool--

00:23:04.360 --> 00:23:06.580
no coding skills required.

00:23:06.580 --> 00:23:08.800
And to show you
more about that, let

00:23:08.800 --> 00:23:10.390
me introduce Vaidy
Krishnan who's

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:12.790
going to come to
stage and show you

00:23:12.790 --> 00:23:16.540
how GCP customers are using
Tableau to gain insights

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:17.957
about their big data.

00:23:17.957 --> 00:23:19.040
VAIDY KRISHNAN: Thank you.

