00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:44.990
And I'll talk about some big
customers wins we've had,

00:24:44.990 --> 00:24:49.700
including several that were
featured on the keynote stage

00:24:49.700 --> 00:24:50.780
yesterday.

00:24:50.780 --> 00:24:55.670
So here's what the
Google Tableau data

00:24:55.670 --> 00:24:57.210
platform looks like.

00:24:57.210 --> 00:25:01.910
So Tableau is just the top
layer of a very deep stack

00:25:01.910 --> 00:25:06.500
that most customers use
to organize and analyze

00:25:06.500 --> 00:25:09.020
information inside
their enterprises.

00:25:09.020 --> 00:25:13.430
So the way this works in most
cases, you have some raw data.

00:25:13.430 --> 00:25:16.660
This could be coming from
sensors, this could be sales,

00:25:16.660 --> 00:25:18.380
this could be marketing data.

00:25:18.380 --> 00:25:20.840
It goes into Google
Cloud Storage.

00:25:20.840 --> 00:25:24.200
Then it flows into one of
many different databases,

00:25:24.200 --> 00:25:29.330
all of which Tableau offers
direct native connectivity to.

00:25:29.330 --> 00:25:32.404
So we're talking Cloud SQL.

00:25:32.404 --> 00:25:34.820
All the marketing website data
goes into Google Analytics,

00:25:34.820 --> 00:25:38.190
Google Sheets, Google BigQuery.

00:25:38.190 --> 00:25:42.140
We'll soon offer direct
connectivity to Google Bigtable

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:45.620
and to Cloud Dataproc, which
is Google's Hadoop as a service

00:25:45.620 --> 00:25:46.226
platform.

00:25:46.226 --> 00:25:47.600
Now, many of you
might ask, well,

00:25:47.600 --> 00:25:49.820
what is native connectivity?

00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:52.580
So native connectivity--
essentially,

00:25:52.580 --> 00:25:55.850
Tableau and Google
working together

00:25:55.850 --> 00:25:59.840
to make sure our connection
is tuned for performance

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:03.920
and makes full use of
the Tableau feature set.

00:26:03.920 --> 00:26:10.010
When we just connect via ODBC,
it's not always optimized,

00:26:10.010 --> 00:26:13.250
so both our engineers
have been hard at work

00:26:13.250 --> 00:26:15.620
to make sure this
integration is performing

00:26:15.620 --> 00:26:17.458
and works great
for our customers.

00:26:22.340 --> 00:26:24.545
So we're talking about
Google BigQuery today.

00:26:24.545 --> 00:26:27.350
I want to spend a
couple of moments just

00:26:27.350 --> 00:26:34.370
to emphasize why our customers
like Google BigQuery.

00:26:34.370 --> 00:26:36.290
And I'm not going
to dwell on this

00:26:36.290 --> 00:26:38.570
because I'm sure many
of you have attended

00:26:38.570 --> 00:26:40.430
several other sessions
that cover this,

00:26:40.430 --> 00:26:42.590
but what I liked the
most about this slide

00:26:42.590 --> 00:26:48.530
is GBQ's doing away with all
the traditional issues you faced

00:26:48.530 --> 00:26:51.860
with data warehouses
on the left-hand side.

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:55.830
You don't have to design,
build, and manage servers.

00:26:55.830 --> 00:27:01.340
You don't have to forecast
capacity, and when

00:27:01.340 --> 00:27:06.440
you do that there's typically
a lot of costs associated

00:27:06.440 --> 00:27:10.760
with it, which creates
a barrier to entry.

00:27:10.760 --> 00:27:14.040
With Google BigQuery, you have
a server-less architecture,

00:27:14.040 --> 00:27:17.990
so it's completely abstracted
out from the customer.

00:27:17.990 --> 00:27:20.450
You can automatically
scale storage up and down,

00:27:20.450 --> 00:27:24.680
based as your needs
and your usage grow.

00:27:24.680 --> 00:27:33.680
And totality-- what this means
is the customer gets agility.

00:27:33.680 --> 00:27:36.960
They don't have to
necessarily invest

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:49.127
upfront and deal with the
risk of just letting their--

00:27:49.127 --> 00:27:50.960
they don't have to deal
with a lot of risks.

00:27:50.960 --> 00:27:57.170
So as with any cloud
platform you have out there,

00:27:57.170 --> 00:27:59.120
Google BigQuery
makes sure that they

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:00.950
don't have to invest
time in capex,

00:28:00.950 --> 00:28:03.680
and most is opex and
scalable and agile.

00:28:06.320 --> 00:28:08.870
So let's talk about some
of the common problems

00:28:08.870 --> 00:28:11.550
you face with analyzing data.

00:28:11.550 --> 00:28:14.786
Well, first is, as with
many legacy BU products--

00:28:14.786 --> 00:28:16.160
and, in fact, a
surprising number

00:28:16.160 --> 00:28:19.610
of new vendors, especially
in the data discovery space--

00:28:19.610 --> 00:28:23.390
they make you write
code to connect to data.

00:28:23.390 --> 00:28:27.170
Which is great for a DBA or
someone who is a developer,

00:28:27.170 --> 00:28:31.030
but if you're a business
user or someone who

00:28:31.030 --> 00:28:35.406
is close to the action
that come out of his work

00:28:35.406 --> 00:28:36.780
and who knows the
data, you don't

00:28:36.780 --> 00:28:39.430
have to spend time doing that.

00:28:39.430 --> 00:28:41.740
Now, once you're able
to connect to data,

00:28:41.740 --> 00:28:44.350
it's typically very
hard to create a vis.

00:28:44.350 --> 00:28:48.890
You need to know
what questions you're

00:28:48.890 --> 00:28:51.140
going to ask before
you analyze it,

00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:53.270
but that's not how
data exploration works.

00:28:53.270 --> 00:28:55.550
90% of the time I just
have a billion rows,

00:28:55.550 --> 00:28:57.980
and I don't even know
what question to ask.

00:28:57.980 --> 00:29:02.090
I need to just explore away
and let that data guide what

00:29:02.090 --> 00:29:04.580
that question should be.

00:29:04.580 --> 00:29:08.750
So imagine a platform
that's asking

00:29:08.750 --> 00:29:12.020
you to click on a
bunch of wizards

00:29:12.020 --> 00:29:13.880
or choose a chart type
even before you've

00:29:13.880 --> 00:29:16.250
asked that question.

00:29:16.250 --> 00:29:21.740
We wanted to disrupt
that experience,

00:29:21.740 --> 00:29:24.350
and I'll talk about how
Tableau solves that.

00:29:24.350 --> 00:29:27.470
And finally, once you've created
something, you have to share--

00:29:27.470 --> 00:29:30.440
and Tim alluded to
this a little earlier--

00:29:30.440 --> 00:29:34.040
that work with the rest
of your organization.

00:29:34.040 --> 00:29:39.770
This could be internally,
with stakeholders or clients,

00:29:39.770 --> 00:29:43.790
to make sure that that
analysis is acted on.

00:29:43.790 --> 00:29:47.630
And again, that has
typically not been easy

00:29:47.630 --> 00:29:49.590
in traditional BI platforms.

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:51.780
So how can Tableau help?

00:29:51.780 --> 00:29:57.650
Well, first of all, you can
connect to all the Google data

00:29:57.650 --> 00:30:01.670
sources I talked about and
a whole slew of other data

00:30:01.670 --> 00:30:03.480
sources in a minute.

00:30:03.480 --> 00:30:08.030
And I firmly believe that
across most organizations,

00:30:08.030 --> 00:30:10.749
not all your data
rests in one place.

00:30:10.749 --> 00:30:12.290
So you may have a
ton of data in GBQ,

00:30:12.290 --> 00:30:14.706
but you have data in Salesforce,
you have data in Marketo.

00:30:14.706 --> 00:30:16.640
You still have some of
those old spreadsheets

00:30:16.640 --> 00:30:20.390
that some of your
teams may be continuing

00:30:20.390 --> 00:30:22.250
to update on an
ongoing basis that you

00:30:22.250 --> 00:30:23.910
want to bring into an analysis.

00:30:26.600 --> 00:30:30.620
We allow you to bring all
those data sources together,

00:30:30.620 --> 00:30:33.470
and we are not just
a visualization tool.

00:30:33.470 --> 00:30:36.320
So I know when we
started out back

00:30:36.320 --> 00:30:38.660
in 2003, the first
couple of years

00:30:38.660 --> 00:30:41.120
the market saw us as a shiny
new toy, but over the years

00:30:41.120 --> 00:30:44.030
we've expanded our capabilities.

00:30:44.030 --> 00:30:47.300
We're now proud to be an
enterprise-ready, mission

00:30:47.300 --> 00:30:50.060
critical platform
that allows people not

00:30:50.060 --> 00:30:51.950
just to analyze
and explore data,

00:30:51.950 --> 00:30:57.860
but to share, govern, and
collaborate and distribute it

00:30:57.860 --> 00:31:03.290
in a way that makes
people feel empowered.

00:31:03.290 --> 00:31:11.180
And IT can also
participate now in driving

00:31:11.180 --> 00:31:13.130
the analytics direction
of organizations.

00:31:13.130 --> 00:31:16.190
So your business
users are governed

00:31:16.190 --> 00:31:18.530
without feeling that
they are being governed,

00:31:18.530 --> 00:31:22.370
and IT is now able to
focus all this time

00:31:22.370 --> 00:31:25.220
on high-level security
and data management tasks.

00:31:30.520 --> 00:31:32.830
So how do Tableau and
Google work together?

00:31:32.830 --> 00:31:35.980
Again, it comes down to three
things-- speed and agility.

00:31:35.980 --> 00:31:38.710
We deploy on the
Google Cloud engine.

00:31:38.710 --> 00:31:40.810
You can connect in minutes,
start analyzing data

00:31:40.810 --> 00:31:42.250
right away.

00:31:42.250 --> 00:31:45.370
GCP offered scale and agility.

00:31:45.370 --> 00:31:47.297
There's no guessing
capacity issues.

00:31:47.297 --> 00:31:48.880
You don't have to
forecast in advance.

00:31:48.880 --> 00:31:51.460
Scale up and down as you need.

00:31:51.460 --> 00:31:56.410
And Tableau works with all kinds
of data and unlimited amounts,

00:31:56.410 --> 00:32:00.910
and I'll illustrate that in a
demonstration in a little bit.

00:32:00.910 --> 00:32:06.540
And of course, there's
TCO, which both of us

00:32:06.540 --> 00:32:09.640
are really great at.

00:32:09.640 --> 00:32:13.270
Ours has been ranked the lowest
among all the major BI vendors,

00:32:13.270 --> 00:32:17.470
and GCP can be up to 40%
cheaper than cloud competitors.

00:32:17.470 --> 00:32:19.840
So what are some best practices
for Tableau and BigQuery

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:20.694
together?

00:32:20.694 --> 00:32:22.610
Again, I'm not going to
dwell on all of these,

00:32:22.610 --> 00:32:25.810
but there's a few
ones that I would

00:32:25.810 --> 00:32:29.140
like to just double-click on.

00:32:29.140 --> 00:32:33.520
And so the top half is
all the best practices

00:32:33.520 --> 00:32:35.740
that have to deal
with Google BigQuery.

00:32:35.740 --> 00:32:39.100
So first of all, when
a visualization is not

00:32:39.100 --> 00:32:44.100
performing, 50% of the
time-- or I would say 70%,

00:32:44.100 --> 00:32:45.060
75% of the time--

00:32:45.060 --> 00:32:47.100
you haven't tuned
your data source well.

00:32:47.100 --> 00:32:49.720
It's a data source that
performs against a data source,

00:32:49.720 --> 00:32:52.390
I always like to
tell my customers.

00:32:52.390 --> 00:32:57.410
If your SQL against a data
source is taking 50 seconds,

00:32:57.410 --> 00:32:59.670
Tableau cannot make
that 40 seconds.

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:02.570
So you always have to start
with the data source first.

00:33:02.570 --> 00:33:03.370
So what do you do?

00:33:03.370 --> 00:33:04.560
Avoid select star.

00:33:04.560 --> 00:33:05.140
Why is that?

00:33:05.140 --> 00:33:08.900
Because Google BigQuery
is a columnar store,

00:33:08.900 --> 00:33:10.540
so what that means
is every column

00:33:10.540 --> 00:33:15.470
is in a different
physical block.

00:33:15.470 --> 00:33:20.870
So if you choose your columns
and not do select stars,

00:33:20.870 --> 00:33:23.010
queries are going to
be more performant.

00:33:23.010 --> 00:33:27.250
Denormalization is
your best friend.

00:33:27.250 --> 00:33:30.200
So Google BigQuery
is great at joins,

00:33:30.200 --> 00:33:34.700
but if you're able to pre-join
and flatten out your structures

00:33:34.700 --> 00:33:42.514
and essentially trade your
storage for compute resources,

00:33:42.514 --> 00:33:44.930
well, obviously you're going
to be paying more for storage

00:33:44.930 --> 00:33:47.334
but paying a lot
less for compute.

00:33:47.334 --> 00:33:49.250
And I think that's a
very effective trade-off.

00:33:49.250 --> 00:33:51.620
Your query's are going
to come back faster.

00:33:51.620 --> 00:33:53.780
So always denormalize
your tables.

00:33:53.780 --> 00:33:56.240
And actually, this works
for Tableau as well.

00:33:56.240 --> 00:33:58.280
We like denormalized
structures, because it

00:33:58.280 --> 00:34:02.780
helps us fire that analytical
query a lot better.

00:34:02.780 --> 00:34:06.530
Leverage natural partitioning
or use explicit charting.

00:34:06.530 --> 00:34:08.900
So again, the point
we're trying to make

00:34:08.900 --> 00:34:14.510
is avoid full table scans and
leverage natural partitioning,

00:34:14.510 --> 00:34:17.300
which is usually your
record create date.

00:34:17.300 --> 00:34:19.130
So if you have a bunch
of tables and you

00:34:19.130 --> 00:34:24.229
have one table for each
date, you can use that.

00:34:24.229 --> 00:34:26.480
Or make sure you're
using some sort of filter

00:34:26.480 --> 00:34:32.000
to not scan everything and
just scan what you need.

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:37.610
On the Tableau side,
again, I have seen billions

00:34:37.610 --> 00:34:38.779
of rows worth of data--

00:34:42.679 --> 00:34:45.290
people experience 20x
performance improvements

00:34:45.290 --> 00:34:47.120
over several billions
of rows of data

00:34:47.120 --> 00:34:50.630
just because they had a solid
indexing and partitioning

00:34:50.630 --> 00:34:52.810
strategy.

00:34:52.810 --> 00:34:54.570
Avoid too many filters.

00:34:54.570 --> 00:34:57.430
Avoid large dataset to
second dataset blending.

00:34:57.430 --> 00:35:01.070
The thing about Tableau
is just because its easy,

00:35:01.070 --> 00:35:03.187
it allows people to
do a lot of things.

00:35:03.187 --> 00:35:04.770
And just because you
can do it doesn't

00:35:04.770 --> 00:35:09.500
mean you should, so avoid that.

00:35:09.500 --> 00:35:13.580
And stay away from
having a large number

00:35:13.580 --> 00:35:16.040
of schemas and columns.

00:35:16.040 --> 00:35:18.230
It's always good
to know in advance

00:35:18.230 --> 00:35:21.800
or try to figure out what
are the columns you want

00:35:21.800 --> 00:35:23.410
to explore, versus what's junk.

00:35:23.410 --> 00:35:28.730
And only connect to
what you know for sure

00:35:28.730 --> 00:35:31.120
you're going to use.

00:35:31.120 --> 00:35:33.870
So with that, let's get
into a really quick demo.

00:35:37.750 --> 00:35:40.460
Here I am connected
to Tableau desktop.

00:35:40.460 --> 00:35:47.770
So this is what an analyst would
use to create visualizations.

00:35:47.770 --> 00:35:52.450
So this is the analyst, and I
am going to go ahead and connect

00:35:52.450 --> 00:35:53.725
to Google BigQuery instance.

00:36:05.581 --> 00:36:07.830
And I want to highlight some
of the improvements we've

00:36:07.830 --> 00:36:15.490
made recently in
support of some features

00:36:15.490 --> 00:36:17.460
that the Google
BigQuery team developed.

00:36:17.460 --> 00:36:21.030
The first thing is, since
GBQ charges for storage

00:36:21.030 --> 00:36:25.320
and computes
separately, you can now

00:36:25.320 --> 00:36:30.650
use the billing project
feature to point

00:36:30.650 --> 00:36:33.530
or to charge a certain project.

00:36:33.530 --> 00:36:37.880
So imagine you have a bunch
of stakeholders or clients.

00:36:37.880 --> 00:36:40.700
You can actually
charge all the queries

00:36:40.700 --> 00:36:43.250
to that one project, which
saves a lot of headache

00:36:43.250 --> 00:36:44.610
down the road.

00:36:44.610 --> 00:36:48.140
The second thing is we
have now the ability

00:36:48.140 --> 00:36:51.786
to write custom SQL, which
can be validated right away.

00:36:51.786 --> 00:36:53.410
Why would you want
to write custom SQL?

00:36:53.410 --> 00:36:55.544
A bunch of different reasons.

00:36:55.544 --> 00:36:56.960
Sometimes, for
instance, you might

00:36:56.960 --> 00:36:59.420
want to use a native
Google BigQuery function.

00:36:59.420 --> 00:37:03.254
Let's say you want to use
the wildcard function.

00:37:03.254 --> 00:37:04.670
How many of you
are aware of that,

00:37:04.670 --> 00:37:07.850
where you can actually do
select star from table dot star?

00:37:07.850 --> 00:37:09.620
So if you want to do
something like that,

00:37:09.620 --> 00:37:12.170
you can leverage custom SQL.

00:37:12.170 --> 00:37:15.620
Also, we have
added a little flag

00:37:15.620 --> 00:37:20.510
that lets you control whether
you want to send a legacy SQL

00:37:20.510 --> 00:37:21.420
or standard SQL.

00:37:21.420 --> 00:37:28.250
So GBQ has now announced
support for standard SQL,

00:37:28.250 --> 00:37:31.160
which is ANSI SQL compliant.

00:37:31.160 --> 00:37:33.170
But there are
several customers who

00:37:33.170 --> 00:37:38.480
have views that have all
been written in legacy SQL.

00:37:38.480 --> 00:37:40.772
So when that's the
case, you obviously

00:37:40.772 --> 00:37:42.980
want to send legacy SQL,
and we give you that option.

00:37:46.151 --> 00:37:50.050
So let's go ahead
and do some analysis.

00:37:50.050 --> 00:37:52.910
So here I am going to pull--

00:37:52.910 --> 00:37:56.480
I think it's about six or seven
years worth of New York City

00:37:56.480 --> 00:38:01.700
taxi data all downloaded
from the NYC.gov website

00:38:01.700 --> 00:38:04.280
and staged into Google
query, and I'm going

00:38:04.280 --> 00:38:05.640
to start asking some questions.

00:38:05.640 --> 00:38:07.306
The first thing I
want to know is, well,

00:38:07.306 --> 00:38:10.170
how many rows there are.

00:38:10.170 --> 00:38:10.950
And let's do that.

00:38:10.950 --> 00:38:13.850
It looks like there's
about a little

00:38:13.850 --> 00:38:16.550
in excess of a billion rows.

00:38:16.550 --> 00:38:21.080
And this is a perfect
example of an analysis

00:38:21.080 --> 00:38:23.565
I'm going to do where I
don't even know what to ask.

00:38:23.565 --> 00:38:25.800
Well, I'm just going
to explore away.

00:38:25.800 --> 00:38:27.560
So first thing I want
to know is, well,

00:38:27.560 --> 00:38:31.400
how have these number of
records trended over time?

00:38:31.400 --> 00:38:42.820
So how about I look at this
by the pickup date time.

00:38:42.820 --> 00:38:44.650
And I'm going to
look at this by year.

00:38:44.650 --> 00:38:47.050
Well, that's kind of nice.

00:38:47.050 --> 00:38:51.060
What if I want to look at
it by quarter or month?

00:38:51.060 --> 00:38:53.340
Maybe I want to express this
a little bit differently

00:38:53.340 --> 00:38:55.157
as a bar.

00:38:55.157 --> 00:38:56.740
The other question
I might want to ask

00:38:56.740 --> 00:39:03.220
is, well, how have the
volumes of rides changed

00:39:03.220 --> 00:39:04.810
over time by payment type?

00:39:04.810 --> 00:39:11.530
So that's one dimension
that I see in the data

00:39:11.530 --> 00:39:15.464
I have, so I can
bring that to color.

00:39:15.464 --> 00:39:17.130
And it's going to
fire that query again.

00:39:22.260 --> 00:39:24.380
And hopefully, it
comes back in a bit.

00:39:24.380 --> 00:39:26.950
Now, while that
happens, let me also

00:39:26.950 --> 00:39:32.700
tell you that Tableau takes a
hybrid approach towards data.

00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:35.160
So we allow our customers
to connect live,

00:39:35.160 --> 00:39:40.290
but customers are
always encouraged

00:39:40.290 --> 00:39:43.830
to use our Tableau data
extract if they only

00:39:43.830 --> 00:39:45.670
want a snapshot in time.

00:39:45.670 --> 00:39:47.484
So they can always
bring data into memory,

00:39:47.484 --> 00:39:48.900
and we have a lot
of customers who

00:39:48.900 --> 00:39:51.420
may have trillions of records.

00:39:51.420 --> 00:39:55.080
But to prove a
concept, they just

00:39:55.080 --> 00:39:57.990
need data for the
last couple of years.

00:39:57.990 --> 00:40:01.320
And you can use a Tableau
data extract to do that,

00:40:01.320 --> 00:40:04.290
so that's nice.

00:40:04.290 --> 00:40:08.190
And what this is doing is
it's highlighting a problem

00:40:08.190 --> 00:40:11.540
that we see with a lot of
data, which is dirty data.

00:40:11.540 --> 00:40:16.110
So here I have cash
transactions being represented

00:40:16.110 --> 00:40:17.570
as multiple categories.

00:40:17.570 --> 00:40:22.852
I might want to just go
ahead and club them as one,

00:40:22.852 --> 00:40:24.310
so I'm going to do
that right here.

00:40:24.310 --> 00:40:27.240
And when I do that, it
doesn't change the source.

00:40:27.240 --> 00:40:29.290
All it's doing is it's
changing the metadata.

00:40:29.290 --> 00:40:31.220
So now we will have
a new payment group

00:40:31.220 --> 00:40:34.230
created when that happens.

00:40:34.230 --> 00:40:38.280
I'm going to go ahead and
club all the credit card

00:40:38.280 --> 00:40:41.982
transactions as one as well.

00:40:41.982 --> 00:40:43.170
So let's do that.

00:40:47.040 --> 00:40:48.710
They're grouping
that all into one.

00:40:58.880 --> 00:41:02.750
And I'm going to keep the
disputed transactions, too.

00:41:02.750 --> 00:41:05.300
So, in fact, I think it might
be useful to club that, too,

00:41:05.300 --> 00:41:09.870
because that is something that
I might want to analyze further.

00:41:16.560 --> 00:41:21.660
So not surprisingly, if you
look at the data from 2009

00:41:21.660 --> 00:41:26.790
through '15, the proportion
of credit card transactions

00:41:26.790 --> 00:41:29.460
is increasing, and it's
not all that surprising.

00:41:29.460 --> 00:41:31.810
But if you wanted to make
the case a lot better,

00:41:31.810 --> 00:41:35.190
you could also add a
little table calculation

00:41:35.190 --> 00:41:38.550
and express this result
as a percentage of total.

00:41:44.680 --> 00:41:49.160
So that just makes it
clearer, a lot nicer.

00:41:49.160 --> 00:41:54.040
What I can also do now is ask a
couple of different questions.

00:41:54.040 --> 00:41:56.380
Maybe I want to look at just
the disputed transactions.

00:41:56.380 --> 00:41:59.740
Maybe I want to know when
do all these disputed

00:41:59.740 --> 00:42:02.050
transactions happen and
where do these happen

00:42:02.050 --> 00:42:08.350
and does that tell
me something about,

00:42:08.350 --> 00:42:13.100
as a cabbie, what are some areas
that I might want to avoid.

00:42:13.100 --> 00:42:17.080
So I'm just going to go
ahead and say, Keep Only,

00:42:17.080 --> 00:42:20.465
and I'm going to clear
this table calculation.

00:42:28.880 --> 00:42:33.050
And I'm going to go ahead
and duplicate the sheet,

00:42:33.050 --> 00:42:36.650
and now I'm going to try to
express this data a little bit

00:42:36.650 --> 00:42:37.370
differently.

00:42:37.370 --> 00:42:41.210
Maybe I want to look at the
same information by hour.

00:42:46.620 --> 00:42:48.480
Again, nothing
surprising-- not a ton

00:42:48.480 --> 00:42:53.430
of rides happening in the
wee hours of the morning,

00:42:53.430 --> 00:42:59.620
but volumes grow as you
get later in the day.

00:42:59.620 --> 00:43:01.900
The next question
is, well, can I

00:43:01.900 --> 00:43:04.510
hone in on certain neighborhoods
or certain boroughs

00:43:04.510 --> 00:43:08.290
within New York where all
these disputes are happening

00:43:08.290 --> 00:43:09.740
the wee hours of the morning?

00:43:09.740 --> 00:43:15.890
So for that, I am going
to represent this data

00:43:15.890 --> 00:43:17.570
geographically.

00:43:17.570 --> 00:43:23.110
So let's look at my
latitudes and longitude data.

00:43:40.230 --> 00:43:41.220
That's what I wanted.

00:43:41.220 --> 00:43:52.430
So there's my latitude, and
let's bring in my longitudes

00:43:52.430 --> 00:43:52.930
here.

00:43:57.261 --> 00:43:59.010
And Tableau has
automatically, by the way,

00:43:59.010 --> 00:44:04.770
geocoded this information,
so it recognizes

00:44:04.770 --> 00:44:08.910
well-described addresses, it
recognizes boroughs in New

00:44:08.910 --> 00:44:12.940
York, it recognizes zip codes.

00:44:12.940 --> 00:44:16.530
But if you don't
have that, you can

00:44:16.530 --> 00:44:21.546
use a whole range of
geocoding resources

00:44:21.546 --> 00:44:22.920
to bring latitudes
and longitudes

00:44:22.920 --> 00:44:24.930
and actually have
them inside Tableau.

00:44:29.295 --> 00:44:32.390
But now you see that
there's some junk in here.

00:44:32.390 --> 00:44:35.120
I mean, some of
those rides have been

00:44:35.120 --> 00:44:37.220
coded as happening in
Africa and Antarctica,

00:44:37.220 --> 00:44:38.870
and obviously I don't want that.

00:44:38.870 --> 00:44:43.460
So I'm just going to go ahead
and hone in on just those rides

00:44:43.460 --> 00:44:45.670
that are coded correctly.

00:44:45.670 --> 00:44:52.850
And that's a lot of rides, so
why don't we focus in and just

00:44:52.850 --> 00:44:56.350
clean this up a little bit.

00:44:56.350 --> 00:44:58.555
And I can change the
map layer as well.

00:45:02.670 --> 00:45:03.300
That's nicer.

00:45:03.300 --> 00:45:08.482
And I can change the opacity,
just so it's nicer to look at.

00:45:08.482 --> 00:45:14.929
And now I can bring this
all into a dashboard

00:45:14.929 --> 00:45:16.720
and actually connect
these different views.

00:45:23.115 --> 00:45:23.865
I don't need that.

00:45:30.740 --> 00:45:39.120
I can go ahead and make
sure it's laid out right,

00:45:39.120 --> 00:45:42.410
and I can enable action filters.

00:45:49.740 --> 00:45:53.970
And with that, I can use one
view to filter the other.

00:45:53.970 --> 00:45:57.490
So obviously, we
see here there is

00:45:57.490 --> 00:45:59.460
a lot of activity
around LaGuardia,

00:45:59.460 --> 00:46:04.200
JFK, some busy thoroughfares
around Brooklyn

00:46:04.200 --> 00:46:06.180
and Williamsburg.

00:46:06.180 --> 00:46:08.627
And Midtown is
always crazy, even

00:46:08.627 --> 00:46:09.960
in the wee hours of the morning.

00:46:09.960 --> 00:46:16.590
So again, just a very quick
illustration of how all of this

00:46:16.590 --> 00:46:17.220
comes together.

00:46:17.220 --> 00:46:21.750
And, again, this was done
off of a billion rows.

00:46:21.750 --> 00:46:27.950
So how are customers leveraging
Tableau and BigQuery together?

00:46:27.950 --> 00:46:30.375
So here's three
different examples.

00:46:30.375 --> 00:46:35.000
We're a fairly
horizontal platform.

00:46:35.000 --> 00:46:38.510
We have customers across
all verticals using Tableau.

00:46:38.510 --> 00:46:41.150
We're in about 95% of the
Fortune 500 companies.

00:46:41.150 --> 00:46:42.620
But the three I
want to highlight

00:46:42.620 --> 00:46:44.510
are one, which is Kabam--

00:46:44.510 --> 00:46:47.810
very small gaming
company, lots of data.

00:46:47.810 --> 00:46:51.560
They've had a relationship
with Google for years,

00:46:51.560 --> 00:46:54.920
and they're asking questions
around their gaming data.

00:46:54.920 --> 00:46:58.610
They want to know
user behavior, where

00:46:58.610 --> 00:47:02.480
do people get stuck,
how do I design

00:47:02.480 --> 00:47:03.830
the game so people spend more.

00:47:03.830 --> 00:47:05.990
These are the kinds of
questions they're asking.

00:47:05.990 --> 00:47:07.987
You have University
of Colorado at Denver,

00:47:07.987 --> 00:47:10.070
and they just came out
with a press release today.

00:47:10.070 --> 00:47:13.249
They're bringing
in their EHR data,

00:47:13.249 --> 00:47:15.290
combining with patient
data and environment data,

00:47:15.290 --> 00:47:17.990
to deliver
personalized medicine.

00:47:17.990 --> 00:47:20.240
And of course, I'm not going
to talk about Home Depot.

00:47:20.240 --> 00:47:25.950
You guys heard from them on
the keynote stage yesterday.

00:47:25.950 --> 00:47:30.110
So with that, just
before I end, here

00:47:30.110 --> 00:47:37.381
are some resources for you guys
on how the two platforms work

00:47:37.381 --> 00:47:37.880
together.

00:47:37.880 --> 00:47:39.671
If you have some
product-related questions,

00:47:39.671 --> 00:47:41.930
please feel free
to reach out to me.

00:47:41.930 --> 00:47:44.600
And if you want
to just try this,

00:47:44.600 --> 00:47:48.760
just go ahead and download
a trial of Tableau

00:47:48.760 --> 00:47:50.534
and a trial of Google BigQuery.

00:47:50.534 --> 00:47:51.200
Connect the two.

00:47:51.200 --> 00:47:53.033
There's a whole bunch
of public data sources

00:47:53.033 --> 00:47:56.450
out there that Tim
pointed you guys to.

00:47:56.450 --> 00:47:59.420
All that New York data
is available on the web.

00:47:59.420 --> 00:48:02.930
There's some Medicare data,
and you can explore them

00:48:02.930 --> 00:48:05.690
to your heart's content.

00:48:05.690 --> 00:48:09.957
Also, check out the white paper.

00:48:09.957 --> 00:48:11.540
I may or may not
have talked about it,

00:48:11.540 --> 00:48:15.870
but all the best
practices that I covered

00:48:15.870 --> 00:48:19.905
have been detailed
in the white paper.

00:48:19.905 --> 00:48:22.530
And there's a lot of examples in
there as well for those of you

00:48:22.530 --> 00:48:24.780
who are existing customers
and have been using the two

00:48:24.780 --> 00:48:26.370
platforms together for years.

